### Poll Messages Structure (Rust)

Source: https://iggy.apache.org/docs/server/schema

Defines the structure for polling messages from a topic partition. It includes consumer details, stream and topic IDs, an optional partition ID, a polling strategy, the number of messages to retrieve, and an auto-commit flag. The polling strategy can be based on offset, timestamp, first, last, or next message.

```rust
pub struct PollMessages {
    pub consumer: Consumer,
    pub stream_id: Identifier,
    pub topic_id: Identifier,
    pub partition_id: Option<u32>,
    pub strategy: PollingStrategy,
    pub count: u32,
    pub auto_commit: bool,
}

pub struct PollingStrategy {
    pub kind: PollingKind,
    pub value: u64,
}

pub enum PollingKind {
    Offset,
    Timestamp,
    First,
    Last,
    Next,
}

// Code: 100
// consumer - variable (Consumer)
// stream_id - variable (Identifier)
// topic_id - variable (Identifier)
// partition_id - 5 bytes (1 byte flag + 4 bytes u32; flag: 1=Some, 0=None)
// strategy - 9 bytes (1 byte kind + 8 bytes u64 value)
// count - 4 bytes
// auto_commit - 1 byte
```

--------------------------------

### Consume Messages from Iggy Stream

Source: https://iggy.apache.org/docs/introduction/getting-started

This snippet demonstrates how to poll messages from a specific stream, topic, and partition using a loop. It handles message processing and offset management to ensure continuous consumption.

```rust
let mut offset = 0;
let messages_per_batch = 10;
let consumer = Consumer::default();
loop {
    let polled_messages = client
        .poll_messages(
            &STREAM_NAME.try_into()?,
            &TOPIC_NAME.try_into()?,
            Some(PARTITION_ID),
            &consumer,
            &PollingStrategy::offset(offset),
            messages_per_batch,
            false,
        )
        .await?;

    if polled_messages.messages.is_empty() {
        info!("No messages found.");
        sleep(interval).await;
        continue;
    }

    offset += polled_messages.messages.len() as u64;
    for message in polled_messages.messages {
        handle_message(&message)?;
    }
    sleep(interval).await;
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Iggy Consumer Group

Source: https://iggy.apache.org/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk

Configures a high-level Iggy consumer for a specific consumer group, stream, and topic. It includes settings for auto-commit, joining the consumer group, polling strategy, and batch length. Dependencies include IggyDuration and AutoCommit configurations.

```rust
let mut consumer = client
    .consumer_group("my-consumer-group", "my-stream", "my-topic")?
    .auto_commit(AutoCommit::IntervalOrWhen(
        IggyDuration::from_str("1s")?,
        AutoCommitWhen::ConsumingAllMessages,
    ))
    .create_consumer_group_if_not_exists()
    .auto_join_consumer_group()
    .polling_strategy(PollingStrategy::next())
    .poll_interval(IggyDuration::from_str("1ms")?)
    .batch_length(1000)
    .build();
```

--------------------------------

### ConsumerOffsetInfo Structure and Serialization

Source: https://iggy.apache.org/docs/server/schema

Defines the ConsumerOffsetInfo structure, returned by the GetConsumerOffset command. It provides partition ID, current offset, and stored offset information. The serialization format is also detailed.

```rust
pub struct ConsumerOffsetInfo {
    pub partition_id: u32,
    pub current_offset: u64,
    pub stored_offset: u64,
}
```

### Building the consumer

Source: https://iggy.apache.org/docs/introduction/getting-started

The `strategy` parameter defines how messages are polled. The default `Offset` strategy requires the client to track the current offset. Alternatively, the `Next` strategy fetches the next messages based on the server-side consumer offset, which can be manually stored using `store_offset()` or automatically committed using `auto_commit: true`, resulting in at-most-once delivery.

### Connect PGliteWorker in the main script

Source: https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite/blob/main/docs/docs/multi-tab-worker.md

This snippet shows how to connect the `PGliteWorker` to the worker process in your main script. The `pg` instance will have the same interface as a standard PGlite interface.

```javascript
import { PGliteWorker } from '@electric-sql/pglite/worker'

const pg = new PGliteWorker(
  new Worker(new URL('./my-pglite-worker.js', import.meta.url), {
    type: 'module',
  }),
)

// `pg` has the same interface as a standard PGlite interface
```

--------------------------------

### Close the database connection

Source: https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite/blob/main/docs/docs/api.md

Call the `close` method to shut down the database cleanly and release resources. This should be done when the application no longer needs database access.

```typescript
await pg.close()
```

--------------------------------

### close

Source: https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite/blob/main/docs/docs/api.md

Closes the database connection, ensuring all resources are released cleanly.

```APIDOC
## close

### Description
Closes the database connection, performing any necessary cleanup to ensure a clean shutdown.

### Method Signature
`.close(): Promise<void>`

### Returns
- `Promise<void>` - A promise that resolves when the database has been closed successfully.

### Example
```ts
await pg.close()
```
```

--------------------------------

### Initialize PGliteWorker in a separate JS file

Source: https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite/blob/main/docs/docs/multi-tab-worker.md

This code sets up the worker file for PGlite. It uses the `worker` wrapper with an `init` option to create and return a PGlite instance, which will be exposed to all tabs.

```javascript
// my-pglite-worker.js
import { PGlite } from '@electric-sql/pglite'
import { worker } from '@electric-sql/pglite/worker'

worker({
  async init() {
    // Create and return a PGlite instance
    return new PGlite()
  },
})
```

--------------------------------

### waitReady and close

Source: https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite/blob/main/src/pglite.ts

waitReady is a public readonly Promise resolved by the async #init() call in the constructor. close() gracefully terminates the database by sending end protocol, running atexit handlers, closing the filesystem, and force-exiting the Emscripten runtime.

```typescript
readonly waitReady: Promise<void>

...

// Initialize the database, and store the promise so we can wait for it to be ready
this.waitReady = this.#init(options ?? {})

...

async close() {
  await this._checkReady()
  this.#closing = true

  // Close all extensions
  for (const closeFn of this.#extensionsClose) {
    await closeFn()
  }

  // Close the database
  try {
    this.mod!._pgl_setPGliteActive(0)
    await this.execProtocol(serialize.end())
    this.mod!._pgl_run_atexit_funcs()
  } catch (e: any) {
    const err = e as { name: string; status: number }
    if (err.name === 'ExitStatus' && err.status === 0) {
      // Database closed successfully
    } else {
      this.#log(`An error occured while closing the db`, e.toString())
    }
  } finally {
    this.mod!.removeFunction(this.#pglite_socket_read)
    this.mod!.removeFunction(this.#pglite_socket_write)
  }

  // Close the filesystem
  await this.fs!.closeFs()

  this.#closed = true
  this.#closing = false
  this.#ready = false
  this.#running = false

  try {
    this.mod!._emscripten_force_exit(0)
  } catch (e: any) {
    this.#log(e)
    if (e.status !== 0) {
      this.#log('Error when exiting', e.toString())
    }
  } finally {
    this.mod = undefined
  }
}
```

